<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SharingRules xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT AND MUST SIT INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT - a comment placed
        ABOVE <SharingRules> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        FSD Gap Tranche 1, Item 1 follow-up (2026-08-16). The design gate flagged that
        OfferingService.ensureOnPsaExecuted (developer work, same PR) creates an Offering__c
        shell on PSA execution that is invisible to the Investor Relations team: Offering__c is
        sharingModel Private with zero sharing rules, the new row is owned by whichever
        acquisitions persona executed the PSA, and IR_Owner__c is deliberately left null (it is
        a User lookup and Investor_Relations is a public GROUP, not a user - no IR owner is
        derivable at creation time). The user's explicit directive: "They will be visible to IR
        team, right now give them read/write permission."

        DPEG_IR_Edit / DPEG_IR_View now grant Offering__c object and field permissions (this
        same PR), but object permissions alone do not grant record access on a Private-OWD
        object - this rule is the other half.

        =====================================================================
        OWNER-BASED, NOT CRITERIA-BASED - AND WHY.
        =====================================================================
        Offering__c has NO record types (verified against the object metadata and the org,
        2026-08-16) and no picklist or checkbox that distinguishes "the Offerings IR needs to
        see" from any others - every single row this object will ever hold is created for
        exactly the reason IR needs to see it (OfferingService is its only non-test creator).
        A criteria rule exists to carve out a SUBSET by which KIND of row it is (see
        NDA__c.sharingRules-meta.xml's RecordTypeId-scoped rules for that shape); there is no
        subset to carve here, so a criteria rule would need an artificial always-true condition
        for no benefit over an owner rule.

        The owning population is exactly "whichever acquisitions persona executed the PSA" -
        the field set OfferingService writes contains no owner override, so the row is owned by
        the running user, drawn from the Acquisitions_Analyst role and its subordinates. This
        is the SAME shape five other acquisitions/disposition child objects already use for an
        acquisitions-owned-record-shared-to-a-team rule - BOV_Submission__c, Broker_Listing__c,
        Disposition__c, Disposition_Offer__c and Wire__c all carry a sharingOwnerRules entry
        with sharedFrom roleAndSubordinates Acquisitions_Analyst. This rule is the same
        mechanism pointed at a different sharedTo group (Investor_Relations instead of
        DPEG_Acquisitions_Team), because the population that needs the access is IR, not the
        acquisitions team who already own the rows.

        =====================================================================
        Edit, not Read - matching the user's literal instruction.
        =====================================================================
        The user asked for "read/write permission", so this rule grants Edit. Object CRUD is
        still the ceiling: DPEG_IR_View holders get Read only regardless of this rule, because
        DPEG_IR_View's Offering__c objectPermissions carries allowEdit=false. A second,
        Read-level rule to the same group would be redundant (Salesforce takes the most
        permissive of overlapping rules on one object/group pair) and would only double the
        one-at-a-time sharing-rule deploy hazard below for no behavioural difference - see
        NDA__c.sharingRules-meta.xml's own reasoning for why it DID need two rules (a different
        population, Principals, needing a genuinely lower access level).

        =====================================================================
        OWD VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED.
        =====================================================================
        Offering__c is sharingModel Private with enableSharing true, confirmed in
        objects/Offering__c/Offering__c.object-meta.xml. A sharing rule on a Public
        Read/Write object would be meaningless, so this was checked before the rule was written.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 DEPLOY THIS ONE AT A TIME WITH ANY OTHER PENDING SHARING RULE CHANGE.
        =====================================================================
        A batch deploy of sharing rules rolls ALL of them back in this org - established in
        the 2026-07-22 RBAC build and repeated in NDA__c.sharingRules-meta.xml's own comment.
        This file introduces exactly one rule, so it is a single deploy on its own, but if it
        is bundled with any other sharing-rule change in the same deploy operation, split them.

        🔴 GROUP MEMBERSHIP IS NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA. If Investor_Relations or
        Acquisitions_Analyst-and-subordinates is empty or wrong in the target org, this rule
        grants NOTHING and IR sees zero Offerings - which looks exactly like "no Offering has
        been created yet". Verify membership as a post-deploy gate (mirrors gate G4 in
        agent-output/design-requirements-fsd-tranche-1.md), and verify it by OPENING AN
        OFFERING AS AN IR USER, not as an administrator.

        🔴 RECALCULATION MAY NOT BE INSTANT. Sharing recalculation runs in the background after
        deploy; on an org with existing Offering__c rows a check immediately after deploy may
        not yet reflect the rule. Do not read that as a failed rule until recalculation
        completes.

        ACQUISITIONS ACCESS IS UNAFFECTED. DPEG_Acquisition_Edit / _View already carry
        viewAllRecords on Offering__c, so the buy-side module's own visibility is untouched by
        this rule; it only widens access to Investor_Relations.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 KNOWN, ACCEPTED RESIDUAL - RECORD THIS BEFORE HUNTING THROUGH PERMISSION SETS.
        =====================================================================
        This rule shares an Offering__c only when its OWNER falls inside
        roleAndSubordinates Acquisitions_Analyst. The role hierarchy is DPEG_Principal at the
        top with THREE children - Acquisitions_Analyst, Investor_Relations_Manager,
        Transactions_Coordinator - so this rule covers that one branch and nothing above or
        beside it.

        An Offering__c is owned by whichever user executed the PSA: the record is inserted by
        ContractExecutionService running in that user's context, with no OwnerId set
        explicitly, so ownership is simply "whoever was logged in when the PSA was marked
        Executed."

        ⇒ AN OFFERING CREATED BY A USER OUTSIDE THE Acquisitions_Analyst BRANCH - a principal
        sitting at DPEG_Principal itself, a System Administrator, or anyone in the
        Investor_Relations_Manager or Transactions_Coordinator branches - IS NOT SHARED TO THE
        Investor_Relations GROUP BY THIS RULE, and IR simply cannot see that row. This is a
        REAL PATH, NOT A HYPOTHETICAL ONE: both DPEG_Acquisition_Edit and DPEG_Admin_Access
        grant Contract_Review__c edit, so an administrator running UAT or a data fix, or a
        principal executing a PSA directly, both reach ContractExecutionService and both mint
        an Offering this rule does not reach.

        🔴 THE FAILURE MODE IS SILENT. There is no error, no failed job, and nothing in the UI
        that flags it - the Offering__c row simply does not appear in the IR user's list view
        or search, indistinguishable from "no Offering was created for this deal." A future
        reader chasing "IR cannot see an Offering" should check THE TAB FIRST, THEN THIS RULE.
        Code review C1 (2026-08-16) found this exact tranche shipped with object permissions,
        field permissions and this sharing rule all correctly granted, and NO ROUTE TO THE
        RECORD AT ALL - Investor_Relations.app-meta.xml declared no Offering__c tab and the
        only <tab>Offering__c</tab> grant in permissionsets/ was on DPEG_Admin_Access, so an IR
        user with full access still had nowhere to click. That gap is fixed
        (applications/Investor_Relations.app-meta.xml + the Offering__c tabSettings entry on
        DPEG_App_Investor_Relations, the layer-2 app-visibility set), but a FUTURE tab
        regression is just as silent as a sharing gap and costs nothing to rule out first: it
        is one file to open, versus recalculation, role hierarchy and ownership to reason
        through below. Only once the tab is confirmed present and visible should this rule -
        and DPEG_IR_Edit / DPEG_IR_View's object and field permissions, which are correctly
        granted and are not the usual cause - be re-audited.

        THIS IS A USER DECISION MADE ON 2026-08-16, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. It was offered as a
        Gate-1 choice against a criteria-based alternative that would share every Offering__c
        record regardless of owner, and the user chose to keep the narrower owner-based rule
        and accept this residual rather than widen it. Do not read this comment as a defect in
        the rule - the rule is correct for its intended, and by far most common, population
        (an Acquisitions_Analyst-branch user executing the PSA); this note exists to bound that
        correctness, not to question it.

        REMEDY, IF THIS RESIDUAL EVER NEEDS CLOSING: replace or supplement this
        sharingOwnerRules entry with a sharingCriteriaRules entry that matches ALL Offering__c
        records (an always-true criterion, since the object carries no record type or
        discriminating field - see the "OWNER-BASED, NOT CRITERIA-BASED" section above for why
        that tradeoff was rejected the first time) granting Edit to the same Investor_Relations
        group. That closes the gap for every owner, not only Acquisitions_Analyst and its
        subordinates, at the cost of losing the narrower, ownership-scoped grant.
    -->
    <sharingOwnerRules>
        <fullName>Offering_IR_Visibility</fullName>
        <accessLevel>Edit</accessLevel>
        <label>Offering IR Visibility</label>
        <sharedTo>
            <group>Investor_Relations</group>
        </sharedTo>
        <sharedFrom>
            <roleAndSubordinates>Acquisitions_Analyst</roleAndSubordinates>
        </sharedFrom>
    </sharingOwnerRules>
</SharingRules>
